window.__BASIS_SCENARIOS__ = [{"id":"sc-bank-inflation","category":"Rates","prompt":"You own shares in a major bank. Inflation comes in hotter than expected, and the market now prices a higher chance of a central-bank rate hike. What is the most likely first-order effect on bank stocks specifically?","options":["Banks tend to benefit — higher rates can widen net interest margins","Banks fall one-for-one with bond prices — they trade as bond proxies","No effect — rate changes pass straight through to customers","Banks suffer most, since inflation erodes the value of their loan books"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Higher rates can widen banks’ net interest margins — they earn more on loans relative to what they pay on deposits. So banks often hold up better than long-duration growth stocks when hike odds rise (the offsetting risk is credit losses if the economy slows)."},{"id":"sc-growth-duration","category":"Equities","prompt":"Rates are rising faster than expected. All else equal, which is typically hit hardest?","options":["A high-growth, no-dividend tech company whose value is mostly future earnings","A cash-rich value stock trading on a low multiple of current earnings","A short-dated Treasury bill rolling over within months","A company with steady current cash flows, pricing power and no debt"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Rising rates lower the present value of distant cash flows the most. High-growth stocks are \"long-duration\" equity — their value sits far in the future — so they usually fall more than low-multiple value names when rates jump."},{"id":"sc-curve-invert","category":"Macro","prompt":"The 2-year Treasury yield rises above the 10-year yield — the curve inverts. Historically, what has this most often signalled?","options":["An elevated risk of recession in the next 1–2 years","Imminent booming growth across the economy","A long stretch of permanently falling inflation ahead","A stock market that is all but guaranteed to rise over the next year"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Inversion (short yields above long) has preceded most recent US recessions. It reflects expectations that the central bank will eventually cut rates as growth slows."},{"id":"sc-downgrade","category":"Credit","prompt":"A company whose bonds you hold is downgraded from BBB- to BB+. What happens?","options":["It becomes high yield (\"fallen angel\"); its price likely falls as investors demand more yield","It becomes investment grade, so its price rises as new buyers step in","Nothing changes — ratings are opinions with no effect on pricing","Its coupon automatically increases to compensate holders for the extra risk"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"BBB- is the lowest investment-grade rung; BB+ is high yield. A bond that crosses down is a \"fallen angel\". More perceived default risk means investors demand higher yield, pushing the price down — sometimes amplified by funds forced to sell."},{"id":"sc-fx-differential","category":"FX","prompt":"The US central bank hikes rates while the eurozone holds steady. All else equal, what tends to happen to USD versus EUR?","options":["USD tends to strengthen as higher yields attract capital","USD tends to weaken as the higher rates slow the US economy","No effect — exchange rates move on trade flows, not rates","EUR strengthens because Europe is seen as the safer bloc"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Capital chases higher risk-adjusted yield. A widening rate differential in the dollar’s favour tends to attract flows into USD assets, supporting the dollar (the basis of the interest-rate-parity intuition)."},{"id":"sc-oil-cut","category":"Commodities","prompt":"OPEC unexpectedly announces a large cut to oil production. First-order effect?","options":["Oil prices tend to rise on tighter supply","Oil prices tend to fall as demand adjusts","No change — production cuts are always fully priced in advance","Gold collapses as capital rotates into energy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Less supply against similar demand pushes prices up. Higher oil can also feed through to headline inflation, which is why energy shocks ripple into rates and equities."},{"id":"sc-risk-off","category":"Macro","prompt":"Geopolitical tensions spike and markets turn fearful (\"risk-off\"). Which typically rallies?","options":["Government bonds and gold (classic safe havens)","Small-cap speculative stocks with high beta","Emerging-market currencies backed by commodity exports","High-yield \"junk\" bonds offering the biggest coupons"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In a flight to safety, investors rotate into perceived safe havens — high-quality government bonds, gold, and often the US dollar — while riskier assets sell off."},{"id":"sc-beat-fall","category":"Equities","prompt":"A company beats earnings estimates, yet the stock drops. Most likely reason?","options":["Forward guidance disappointed, or the beat was already priced in","Beating estimates always lowers a stock — good news gets sold","Markets ignore quarterly earnings entirely and trade only on macro data","A law forces companies to cancel dividends after big beats"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Markets are forward-looking. If expectations were already high or management’s outlook is weak, even a beat can disappoint — the classic \"buy the rumour, sell the news\"."},{"id":"sc-cut-bonds","category":"Rates","prompt":"The central bank cuts rates by more than expected. What happens to existing bond prices?","options":["They rise — yields fall and prices move inversely","They fall — cheaper money makes the old bonds obsolete","They stay unchanged until the bonds mature","Their coupons drop to match the new policy rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Bond prices and yields move inversely. Lower market rates make existing higher-coupon bonds more attractive, so their prices rise."},{"id":"sc-strong-usd-mnc","category":"FX","prompt":"The dollar strengthens sharply. A US company that earns most of its revenue abroad will likely report…","options":["Lower revenue/earnings as foreign sales convert into fewer dollars","Higher revenue/earnings as the strong dollar boosts its brand abroad","No FX impact at all — corporate hedging removes it completely","Automatic tariff refunds that offset the currency move"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A stronger dollar means foreign-currency revenue translates back into fewer dollars — a currency headwind for US multinationals (and a tailwind when the dollar weakens)."},{"id":"sc-real-return","category":"Macro","prompt":"Inflation runs at 6% while your savings earn 1% interest. What is happening to your cash?","options":["It is losing purchasing power — a negative real return","It is gaining real value thanks to the interest earned","It is unchanged in real terms — interest offsets inflation","Nothing — inflation does not affect money held in cash"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Real return ≈ nominal rate − inflation. At 1% minus 6%, your cash loses about 5% of its purchasing power a year, even though the nominal balance ticks up."},{"id":"sc-vix","category":"Equities","prompt":"The VIX (the \"fear index\") spikes sharply. What does this indicate?","options":["Expected near-term market volatility has jumped","Stocks are certain to rise over the coming weeks","Interest rates were just cut by the central bank","Trading volume across the market has dried up"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The VIX measures the volatility implied by S&P 500 options. A spike means traders expect bigger swings — it usually rises when markets fall sharply."},{"id":"sc-defensives","category":"Equities","prompt":"A recession looks increasingly likely. Which sector is traditionally considered \"defensive\"?","options":["Consumer staples and utilities","Luxury goods and premium brands","Speculative technology start-ups","Travel, leisure and hospitality"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Defensive sectors sell things people buy regardless of the economy — food, household goods, electricity — so their earnings hold up better in a downturn."},{"id":"sc-duration","category":"Rates","prompt":"You hold a 10-year bond and a 2-year bond. Rates rise 1%. Which loses more value?","options":["The 10-year — it has higher duration","The 2-year — short bonds react faster","They lose exactly the same amount","Neither — the coupons absorb the move"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Longer maturity means higher duration and greater price sensitivity to rates. A 1% rise hits the 10-year far harder than the 2-year."},{"id":"sc-div-cut","category":"Equities","prompt":"A company unexpectedly cuts its dividend. Typical share-price reaction?","options":["It often falls — markets read a cut as a sign of stress","It always rises — the company keeps more cash to grow","No reaction — dividends are already known in advance","It triggers a mandatory stock split under the listing rules"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A dividend cut signals the board is worried about cash or earnings. Income investors may sell, and the market often takes it as a red flag — so the price usually falls."},{"id":"sc-qe","category":"Macro","prompt":"A central bank launches quantitative easing (QE), buying large amounts of bonds. Typical effect?","options":["Bond prices up, yields down, financial conditions eased","Yields rise sharply as more bonds hit the market","The money supply shrinks as bonds leave circulation","The currency always strengthens on central bank buying"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"QE buying pushes bond prices up and yields down, injecting liquidity and easing financial conditions to support the economy. Quantitative tightening (QT) does the reverse."},{"id":"sc-buyback","category":"Equities","prompt":"A company announces a large share buyback. All else equal, what happens?","options":["Shares outstanding fall, so earnings per share can rise","Shares outstanding rise, spreading earnings more thinly","It is economically identical to issuing new equity","The dividend must be cut by law to fund the buyback"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A buyback reduces the share count (the opposite of dilution), concentrating ownership and often lifting EPS — one reason markets frequently react positively."},{"id":"sc-carry","category":"FX","prompt":"An investor borrows in a low-interest-rate currency to invest in a high-interest-rate one, pocketing the difference. This is called…","options":["A carry trade","A hedge","A short squeeze","Arbitrage-free pricing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"That’s a carry trade — earning the interest-rate differential. It works until the funding currency strengthens or volatility spikes, which can unwind it violently."},{"id":"sc-margin-call","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"You are long a futures contract and the market moves against you, draining your margin below the maintenance level. What happens?","options":["A margin call — you must add funds or the position gets closed out","Nothing at all happens until the contract reaches its expiry date","Your coupon is cut until the margin account is rebuilt","You automatically lock in a profit on the original position"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Futures are marked to market daily. If losses pull your margin below the maintenance level, you get a margin call and must top up — or the broker closes the position."},{"id":"sc-rotation","category":"Equities","prompt":"Rates are rising and investors sell expensive growth names to buy cheaper value names. This shift is known as a…","options":["Style rotation (growth-to-value)","A short squeeze","A margin spiral","A sector-wide dividend reinvestment wave"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A style rotation. As the macro regime changes, capital rotates between factors — here from long-duration growth into shorter-duration value, which is less sensitive to rising rates."},{"id":"sc-stock-split","category":"Equities","prompt":"A company announces a 10-for-1 stock split. What happens to the total value of your holding?","options":["Unchanged — you own 10× the shares at 1/10 the price","It rises 10× as each share multiplies","It falls by 90% as the price adjusts","The dividend is cancelled for the whole year of the split"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A split divides the pie into more slices: more shares at a proportionally lower price. Your total value (and the company’s market cap) is unchanged — it just lowers the per-share price."},{"id":"sc-usd-commodities","category":"Commodities","prompt":"The US dollar strengthens sharply. Commodities priced in dollars (like oil) tend to…","options":["Fall — they become more expensive for buyers using other currencies","Rise sharply — a strong dollar signals a booming global economy that needs more oil","Be completely unaffected — supply and demand set prices alone","Always move one-for-one in the same direction as the dollar"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Most commodities are priced in USD. A stronger dollar makes them costlier in other currencies, softening demand — so commodity prices and the dollar often move inversely."},{"id":"sc-fed-pause","category":"Rates","prompt":"The central bank signals it will pause its rate-hiking cycle. Typical first reaction in risk assets?","options":["Equities tend to rally on relief","Equities crash on the loss of support","Bonds sell off hard across the curve","Nothing moves until the next meeting"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A pause removes a headwind: lower expected rates lift the present value of future cash flows, so equities (especially growth) often rally on a dovish pivot."},{"id":"sc-spreads-widen","category":"Credit","prompt":"Corporate credit spreads widen sharply. What is the market signalling?","options":["Rising default/recession risk — investors demand more yield to lend","Falling default risk — companies are becoming safer to lend to","Lower inflation ahead, locked in with certainty","A guaranteed rally once the spreads finish widening"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Widening spreads mean investors want a bigger premium over risk-free bonds to hold corporate credit — a classic sign of rising perceived default risk or recession fear."},{"id":"sc-gold-real-rates","category":"Commodities","prompt":"Real (inflation-adjusted) yields rise sharply. Likely effect on gold, which pays no yield?","options":["Gold tends to fall — the opportunity cost of holding it rises","Gold always rises when real yields rise — they move together","No relationship — gold trades purely on jewellery demand","Gold becomes a yield-bearing asset at high enough rates"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Gold yields nothing, so when real yields on safe bonds rise, holding gold costs more in forgone return — higher real rates are generally a headwind for gold."},{"id":"sc-hike-long-bonds","category":"Rates","prompt":"Inflation surprises high and the central bank hikes more than expected. Long-dated government bonds…","options":["Fall in price — yields rise and long duration amplifies the move","Rise in price as investors seek safety from the inflation","Are unaffected — only short-dated bonds respond to the policy rate","Pay a higher coupon immediately to match the new rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Higher rates push yields up and prices down; long-dated bonds have high duration, so they fall the most. Existing coupons are fixed — only newly issued bonds pay more."},{"id":"sc-tech-miss","category":"Equities","prompt":"A mega-cap tech company misses earnings badly. Likely effect on a tech-heavy index like the Nasdaq?","options":["It drags the index down — the index is concentrated in a few giants","No effect — broad indices diversify away single-stock news","The index actually rises as money rotates into the other 99 constituents","Only bond markets are affected by a single company's miss"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Cap-weighted indices like the Nasdaq are dominated by a handful of mega-caps, so a big miss from one can pull the whole index down."},{"id":"sc-jpy-haven","category":"FX","prompt":"A wave of global risk-off hits markets. Which currency often strengthens as a safe haven?","options":["The Japanese yen (JPY)","An emerging-market currency","A high-yield commodity currency","A frontier-market currency"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The yen (along with the Swiss franc and US dollar) tends to strengthen in risk-off episodes, partly as carry trades funded in yen unwind."},{"id":"sc-flight-quality","category":"Macro","prompt":"Equities sell off hard on recession fears. What typically happens to high-quality government bond prices?","options":["They rise — a \"flight to quality\" pushes yields down","They fall alongside stocks in one broad market liquidation","They become worthless as recession risk rises","Their coupons are cut to reflect the weaker economy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In a flight to quality, investors buy safe government bonds, pushing prices up and yields down — which is why bonds often hedge equity drawdowns."},{"id":"sc-pe-expand","category":"Equities","prompt":"A stock's price rises while its earnings stay flat. Its price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio…","options":["Expands (rises)","Falls","Stays exactly the same","Becomes negative"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"P/E = price ÷ earnings. If price rises and earnings are unchanged, the multiple expands — investors are paying more per £1 of earnings, usually on higher growth expectations."},{"id":"sc-pmi-contraction","category":"Macro","prompt":"The ISM Manufacturing PMI prints at 47 — below 50 for the third consecutive month. What does this signal about the factory sector?","options":["Contraction — activity is shrinking, not just slowing","Expansion at a modest but steady pace","No meaningful change from the prior month's readings","The fastest factory growth on record"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"PMI above 50 means expansion; below 50 means contraction. Three consecutive sub-50 readings suggest a sustained manufacturing downturn, which often leads broader economic weakness by one to two quarters."},{"id":"sc-short-squeeze","category":"Equities","prompt":"A heavily-shorted stock surges 40% on unexpectedly strong earnings. Short sellers scramble to exit. What mechanism amplifies the rally?","options":["Short sellers must buy shares to close positions, driving the price even higher","The company itself steps in and buys back shares in the open market to support the rally","Index funds are forced to rebalance into the rising stock","Options market makers cancel their hedges, adding fresh supply"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A short squeeze occurs when a sharp price rise forces short sellers to buy shares to cover losses, pushing the price up further and triggering more forced buying. The tighter the float and higher the short interest, the more violent the squeeze."},{"id":"sc-rights-issue","category":"Equities","prompt":"A company announces a rights issue, offering existing shareholders new shares at a 20% discount to the current price. What typically happens to the share price immediately?","options":["It falls toward the theoretical ex-rights price, diluting existing holders","It jumps because the company has just raised fresh growth capital","It is unaffected until the day the new shares actually settle","It rises by exactly the 20% discount on the new shares"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The share price adjusts down to the theoretical ex-rights price (TERP) — a weighted average of the old price and the discounted new issue price. While dilutive short-term, rights issues are often positive if proceeds fund growth or reduce dangerous leverage."},{"id":"sc-breakeven-inflation","category":"Rates","prompt":"The 10-year breakeven inflation rate (nominal yield minus TIPS yield) spikes from 2.2% to 2.8%. What are bond investors signalling?","options":["They expect annual inflation to average 2.8% over the next decade","They expect the central bank to cut rates immediately","They believe real economic growth will accelerate sharply","They are pricing in a meaningful chance of government default"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The breakeven rate is the market's implied inflation forecast embedded in bond prices. A spike means the bond market now expects structurally higher inflation — a signal that often precedes pressure on the Fed to tighten monetary policy."},{"id":"sc-cds-sovereign","category":"Credit","prompt":"Credit default swap (CDS) spreads on a country's 5-year government bonds widen from 80 bps to 350 bps in a week. What is the market pricing?","options":["A sharply higher probability of sovereign default or debt restructuring","A sharp drop in interest rates across the curve","Strong GDP growth expectations over the country's next several years","An imminent appreciation of the country's currency"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Sovereign CDS spreads measure the cost of insuring against default. A spike from 80 to 350 bps indicates the market now prices roughly four times the default risk — typically triggered by political instability, fiscal deterioration, or a balance of payments crisis."},{"id":"sc-repo-spike","category":"Rates","prompt":"The overnight repo rate spikes from 2% to 8% intraday, suggesting banks are scrambling for short-term funding. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A shortage of bank reserves, often due to large Treasury settlements or quarter-end demand","The central bank accidentally set its policy rate too low","A surge in consumer spending draining household bank accounts","Companies across the market paying unusually large dividends simultaneously"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Repo market stress often occurs when large Treasury auctions settle (draining reserves) or at quarter-end when banks hoard cash for balance sheet purposes. The September 2019 repo spike in the US forced the Fed to resume balance sheet expansion."},{"id":"sc-hy-spreads","category":"Credit","prompt":"High-yield (junk bond) spreads over Treasuries blow out from 350 bps to 700 bps. What does this mean for companies with upcoming debt maturities?","options":["Refinancing becomes much more expensive — some may struggle to roll debt","They can suddenly borrow much more cheaply than before","Their existing debt is immediately cancelled by the widening","Their equity automatically benefits when the spreads widen"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Wider spreads mean the market demands higher yields to lend to risky borrowers. Companies needing to refinance maturing bonds face much higher interest costs — or may find the market effectively closed. This is a key channel through which credit tightening slows the economy."},{"id":"sc-copper-china","category":"Commodities","prompt":"Copper prices fall 15% after China releases weaker-than-expected manufacturing data. Why does copper track Chinese data so closely?","options":["China consumes roughly half of global copper, so its industrial demand sets the marginal price","China sets the global copper price directly through state control of the exchanges","Copper is priced in Chinese yuan, so currency moves explain the fall","Most of the world's copper mines are located inside China"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"China accounts for about 50% of global copper demand — used in construction, electrical wiring, and manufacturing. Because of this, copper prices are often called \"Dr Copper\" for their predictive power over global industrial activity and Chinese growth."},{"id":"sc-em-currency","category":"FX","prompt":"The US dollar strengthens sharply as the Fed signals higher-for-longer rates. Which emerging market economies are most vulnerable?","options":["Those with large dollar-denominated external debts and thin foreign-currency reserves","Those with large trade surpluses, heavy commodity exports and fast-growing reserves","Those whose currency is pegged to gold rather than to the dollar","Those with the highest GDP growth rates in their region"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"EM countries that borrowed in dollars face a double bind when the dollar strengthens: their debt repayments become more expensive in local currency terms, and capital often flows back to the US for higher yields, further weakening their currencies and straining reserves."},{"id":"sc-curve-steepening","category":"Rates","prompt":"The yield curve bear-steepens: short rates fall modestly while long rates rise sharply. Which investor group is most likely selling long-dated bonds?","options":["Term premium is rising — fiscal concerns or inflation uncertainty pushing long yields up","The central bank, cutting rates aggressively at the short end of the curve","Retail investors rotating out of equities and into bonds","Foreign central banks buying short-dated paper for their reserves"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Bear steepening often signals concerns about long-run inflation or government borrowing. Long-duration bond sellers are demanding more compensation (term premium) to hold debt for 10-30 years, while the front end falls on growth fears. This is often negative for both bonds and equities."},{"id":"sc-real-assets-inflation","category":"Macro","prompt":"Inflation surprises to the upside for several months. Which asset class has historically offered the best protection?","options":["Real assets: commodities, real estate, and inflation-linked bonds (TIPS)","Long-duration government bonds, locked in at yesterday's low yields","Cash held on deposit in a current account","High-growth technology stocks with distant cash flows"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Real assets tend to maintain purchasing power during inflationary periods. Commodities often rise directly with prices; property rents and values inflate alongside; TIPS principal adjusts upward with CPI. Long-duration nominal bonds and cash are the worst inflation hedges."},{"id":"sc-earnings-revision","category":"Equities","prompt":"A company reports in-line Q3 earnings but cuts full-year guidance. The stock falls 12% despite meeting consensus estimates. Why?","options":["Markets are forward-looking — the guidance cut signals future earnings will disappoint","In-line results always cause sell-offs in a nervous market","The company paid no dividend, which triggered institutional selling programs","Guidance cuts are irrelevant noise; only realised earnings can move a price"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Stock prices reflect future expectations, not past results. Meeting last quarter's number is irrelevant if the company is flagging that next quarter and beyond will be weaker. A guidance cut triggers analysts to revise EPS estimates down, repricing the stock at a lower forward multiple."},{"id":"sc-convertible-bond","category":"Credit","prompt":"A company issues a convertible bond with a conversion premium of 30%. The stock must rise 30% above the current price for conversion to make sense. If the stock falls, the bondholder…","options":["Is protected as a creditor and still receives coupons and principal","Loses the entire investment, exactly like an equity holder","Must immediately convert into equity and take the loss","Can force the company to buy back its shares at the old price"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Convertibles combine bond downside protection with equity upside optionality. If the stock stays below the conversion price, the holder simply keeps the bond — collecting coupons and receiving par at maturity. The \"busted convertible\" trades as a straight bond when the equity is far out of the money."},{"id":"sc-tips-real-yield","category":"Rates","prompt":"Real yields on 10-year TIPS fall from +1.5% to -0.5%. Which asset class typically benefits most from deeply negative real yields?","options":["Gold — no income, but no opportunity cost either when real yields are negative","Short-dated corporate bonds rolling at the new rates","Bank savings accounts, which track the policy rate","Financial-sector equities, which earn their living off net interest margins"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Gold has no yield, which is normally a disadvantage versus bonds. But when real bond yields turn negative, the opportunity cost of holding gold disappears — investors aren't giving up positive real income. Negative real yields are historically gold's strongest tailwind."},{"id":"sc-ipo-pop","category":"Equities","prompt":"A highly anticipated tech company IPOs at $20 per share and closes its first day at $38. Who benefits most from this \"pop\"?","options":["The institutions allocated shares at $20 — not the company, and not buyers at the open","The company, which collected the full $38 for every share sold","Retail investors who managed to buy at the $20 offer price","The underwriting banks, who pocket the entire $18-per-share gain as their fee"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"IPO shares are allocated to institutional investors at the offer price before trading begins. The company raises capital at $20; the pop to $38 is a gain for those allocatees, not the issuer. Critics argue large first-day pops mean the company left money on the table by pricing too conservatively."},{"id":"sc-delta-hedging","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"An options market-maker sells a call option with a delta of 0.6. To delta-hedge, they immediately…","options":["Buy 0.6 shares per option sold, so the option and stock P&L offset","Sell 0.6 shares per option, doubling down on the short exposure","Buy another call option with a delta of exactly 0.4 to net out","Wait until expiry before putting on any hedge at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Delta measures how much an option's price moves per $1 move in the underlying. A market-maker short a call with delta 0.6 buys 0.6 shares to stay delta-neutral — gains on the stock offset losses on the option as price moves. This hedge must be rebalanced continuously as delta changes."},{"id":"sc-nat-gas-supply","category":"Commodities","prompt":"A cold snap and pipeline outage hit simultaneously, cutting natural gas supply while demand surges. Prices spike 60% in a week. Why can't other suppliers quickly fill the gap?","options":["Gas infrastructure is fixed in the short run — storage and pipelines can't expand overnight","The government always caps gas prices before they can rise this far","Alternative energy sources instantly scale up to fill any supply gap","Producers voluntarily hold back their supply to keep prices low for long-term customers"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Natural gas markets are highly inelastic in the short run — pipelines, liquefaction plants, and storage are fixed assets that take years to expand. When supply disrupts and demand spikes simultaneously, prices must do all the adjustment work, which is why gas prices can move dramatically and quickly."},{"id":"sc-dividend-recap","category":"Credit","prompt":"A private equity firm causes its portfolio company to borrow $500m and immediately pay it out as a dividend to the PE fund. What risk does this create for the company's existing creditors?","options":["The company's leverage spikes, increasing default risk for all bondholders","Creditors benefit — more cash has just moved through the business","The dividend is deducted from future interest payments automatically","No risk at all — debt covenants always prevent this kind of payout"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A dividend recapitalisation extracts equity value for the PE sponsor while leaving the debt with the company. Leverage jumps, interest coverage falls, and existing bondholders find themselves holding riskier debt than before — often with no compensation. It is a key source of conflict between PE sponsors and credit investors."},{"id":"sc-small-cap-liquidity","category":"Equities","prompt":"A fund manager needs to sell a 5% stake in a small-cap stock quickly. The bid-ask spread is wide and daily trading volume is thin. The main risk is…","options":["Market impact — selling into a thin market moves the price against them","Regulatory intervention blocking the sale of a stake that size","The stock exchange closing early before the order completes","Corporate insiders stepping in to buy the shares at inflated prices"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Liquidity risk in small caps means that selling a large position relative to daily volume moves the market against you. This price impact (market impact cost) can far exceed the bid-ask spread, making the effective exit price much worse than the quoted price. Large institutions often avoid small caps for exactly this reason."},{"id":"sc-m2-surge","category":"Macro","prompt":"M2 money supply grows 25% year-on-year. Classical monetary theory predicts…","options":["Eventual upward pressure on inflation, assuming velocity of money is stable","Immediate deflation as the banks hoard all of the excess money","No effect on prices — only fiscal policy can drive inflation","A guaranteed stock market crash arriving within six months"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The quantity theory of money (MV = PQ) holds that money growth eventually feeds into prices. In practice there are long and variable lags — velocity of money can fall (as in 2020-21 when stimulus was saved). But sustained 25% M2 growth, as seen in 2020-22, eventually showed up as the highest US inflation in 40 years."},{"id":"sc-covenant-breach","category":"Credit","prompt":"A leveraged company's net debt to EBITDA rises to 5.5x, breaching its 5x loan covenant. What power does this give lenders?","options":["The right to demand repayment or renegotiate — real leverage over the borrower","No power — covenants are guidelines rather than legally binding terms","The right to take ownership of the company's brand but not its assets","Automatic conversion of the debt into equity at par value"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Financial covenants are legally binding constraints in loan agreements. A breach is a technical default, giving lenders the right to accelerate (demand immediate full repayment) or extract concessions: higher interest rates, additional collateral, or management changes. This is why covenant headroom is closely watched in leveraged credit analysis."},{"id":"sc-spac","category":"Equities","prompt":"A SPAC (Special Purpose Acquisition Company) raises $300m in an IPO. Investors know only that it will use the money to acquire a private company within 24 months. What is the key risk if no suitable deal is found?","options":["The SPAC must return the cash to investors at par, and the sponsors lose their promote","Investors forfeit their capital entirely, with no recourse against the sponsors at all","The SPAC automatically converts into an operating company chosen by its bankers","The SEC seizes the trust cash and redistributes it directly to retail investors"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"SPACs hold IPO proceeds in a trust. If no deal completes within the deadline (typically 18-24 months), investors vote to liquidate and receive their money back (usually $10 per unit plus interest). Sponsors — who received 20% \"founder shares\" — lose their equity stake. This creates strong sponsor incentive to do a deal, even a suboptimal one."},{"id":"sc-tech-fcf","category":"Equities","prompt":"A large tech platform reports $10bn net income but $15bn free cash flow. Which is the better measure of true economic earnings, and why?","options":["Free cash flow — it strips out non-cash charges like stock-based compensation","Net income — it follows strict GAAP rules and is therefore always more accurate","They are identical by definition for large, capital-light technology companies","Net income, because it properly includes the depreciation of physical assets"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Tech companies often have large stock-based compensation (SBC) charges that reduce GAAP net income but are non-cash. Free cash flow adds back depreciation/amortisation but the treatment of SBC varies. Many analysts prefer unlevered free cash flow (EBITDA – CapEx – working capital changes) for cross-company comparisons as it is harder to manipulate."},{"id":"sc-negative-rates","category":"Rates","prompt":"The European Central Bank sets its deposit rate at -0.5%. What perverse incentive does this create for banks?","options":["Banks are charged for parking excess reserves, pushing them to lend more instead","Banks instantly pass the negative rate to retail depositors, triggering bank runs","Banks earn a guaranteed extra income for every euro they park at the central bank","Negative policy rates mechanically push government bond yields sharply higher"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A negative deposit rate charges banks for parking excess reserves at the central bank — the idea is to push them to lend or invest instead. In practice the effect is limited because banks fear losing depositors if they charge them negative rates, so the penalty is partly absorbed by bank margins."},{"id":"sc-activist-campaign","category":"Equities","prompt":"An activist hedge fund discloses a 7% stake in a conglomerate and publicly calls for a break-up of its divisions. The stock jumps 15%. Why?","options":["Markets expect a break-up to unlock the hidden \"conglomerate discount\" value","Any large investor buying a stake always drives an automatic 15% rise in the shares","The activist fund is now legally required to pay a premium for all remaining shares","Break-ups reliably increase total company debt, which existing bondholders love"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Conglomerates often trade at a discount to the sum of their parts because investors struggle to value diverse businesses and management attention is diluted. An activist campaign proposing a break-up or spin-off signals that hidden value may be unlocked — the market re-rates the stock toward the sum-of-parts valuation."},{"id":"sc-stock-buyback","category":"Equities","prompt":"A company announces a $5bn share buyback. All else equal, what happens to earnings per share (EPS)?","options":["EPS rises — the same earnings are spread over fewer shares","EPS falls — cash leaves the company","EPS is unaffected — buybacks do not touch the income statement","EPS doubles automatically when shares are cancelled"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A buyback reduces the share count. EPS = Net Income ÷ Shares Outstanding. With fewer shares and the same earnings, EPS rises mechanically — which is why buybacks can flatter per-share metrics without improving the underlying business. Critics note executives with stock-based pay have a personal incentive to buy back shares."},{"id":"sc-leverage-roc","category":"Credit","prompt":"A private equity buyout uses 60% debt to acquire a company. If the company's value rises 20%, what happens to the equity return?","options":["The equity return is amplified — leverage multiplies both gains and losses","The equity return matches the 20% asset return almost exactly, dollar for dollar","The debt holders capture the entire gain, since they rank ahead of the equity","The return is reduced, because the interest payments dilute the equity profit"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Leverage amplifies returns on equity. If you put in 40% equity and borrow 60%, a 20% rise in asset value on a $100m deal means $20m gain on $40m of equity — a 50% return. This is why PE uses leverage. The flip side: a 20% fall wipes out half the equity. Leverage is return amplification, not return creation."},{"id":"sc-fx-carry","category":"FX","prompt":"A trader borrows in Japanese yen at near-0% interest and invests in Australian dollars yielding 4.5%. This strategy is called a carry trade. What is its main risk?","options":["A sudden yen rally that makes repaying the yen loan far costlier in AUD terms","The Reserve Bank of Australia cutting its policy rate to below Japanese yen rates","An outright Australian government default on its own domestic and foreign bonds","The Bank of Japan randomly cancelling outstanding yen loans held by foreigners"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The carry trade earns the interest rate differential but is exposed to exchange rate risk. If the yen suddenly strengthens (as it often does in crises, when carry trades unwind), the AUD proceeds convert to fewer yen, potentially wiping out years of carry income. Carry trades tend to go up slowly and unwind violently."},{"id":"sc-credit-cycle","category":"Credit","prompt":"Banks tighten lending standards significantly: requiring higher credit scores, larger down payments, and lower loan-to-value ratios. What macroeconomic effect typically follows?","options":["Credit contraction slows spending and investment, dragging on GDP growth","Tighter standards instantly push interest rates higher across the whole economy","House prices reliably rise when banks tighten, as fewer buyers compete for homes","The central bank is forced to raise its policy rate directly in response to it"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Bank lending is a key transmission mechanism for monetary policy and economic cycles. When banks tighten (whether by choice or regulatory pressure), credit availability shrinks. Households and businesses borrow and spend less, which pulls down aggregate demand. This is why the Fed watches bank lending surveys as a leading economic indicator."},{"id":"sc-gdp-vs-gnp","category":"Macro","prompt":"Ireland's GDP is dramatically higher than its GNP. This unusual gap most likely reflects…","options":["Multinational profits earned in Ireland but repatriated abroad, inflating GDP","Unusually high government spending that only enters the GDP calculation, not GNP","Ireland exporting more physical goods than services, which boosts the GDP measure","GNP fully excluding financial-sector activity, which happens to be large in Ireland"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"GDP counts all output produced within a country's borders; GNP counts output by a country's residents and companies. Ireland hosts huge multinational profits (Apple, Google, pharma) that flow straight out of the country — so they lift Irish GDP but not GNP. Irish economists often use Modified GNI (GNI*) as a more realistic measure of the domestic economy."},{"id":"sc-bond-covenant","category":"Credit","prompt":"A bond indenture includes a \"change of control\" put provision. A private equity firm launches a leveraged buyout of the issuer. What right do bondholders gain?","options":["The right to sell the bonds back to the company at par if the buyout completes","The right to convert the bonds into equity at the original par issue price instead","Automatic cancellation of all outstanding debt the moment control formally changes","The right to appoint a board seat at the newly acquired, more leveraged company"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Change-of-control puts protect bondholders from the risk that new owners (especially PE sponsors) will load up the business with more debt, making existing bonds riskier. If triggered, bondholders can exit at par rather than be stuck holding much riskier paper in a now-leveraged company. Not all bonds include this provision."},{"id":"sc-sector-rotation","category":"Equities","prompt":"The economy is accelerating and inflation is rising. Which sector rotation does the historical playbook suggest?","options":["Into cyclicals and commodities, out of defensives and long-duration growth","Into utilities and consumer staples, the sectors that benefit most from fast growth","Into cash entirely — sell every position and simply wait until inflation has peaked","Into long-dated government bonds, to benefit from steadily falling real yields"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Late-cycle expansion with rising inflation historically favours cyclical sectors whose revenues grow with the economy and commodities producers who benefit from rising prices. Defensive sectors (utilities, healthcare) and long-duration growth stocks underperform as rate expectations rise. The \"sector clock\" is a popular (if imperfect) framework for this."},{"id":"sc-secondary-market","category":"Markets","prompt":"An investor buys Apple shares on the NASDAQ. Does Apple receive any of this money?","options":["No — a secondary trade just transfers shares; Apple was only paid at the IPO","Yes — Apple collects a 1% royalty on every single secondary-market transaction","Yes — all of the proceeds flow to the company, minus the exchange's trading fees","Only when Apple happens to be actively buying back its own shares on that same day"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The primary market is where companies raise money (IPOs, follow-ons). The secondary market — stock exchanges like NASDAQ and NYSE — is where existing shareholders trade with each other. The company receives nothing from secondary trading. This distinction matters: liquid secondary markets make investors willing to buy in primaries, enabling corporate fundraising."},{"id":"sc-esg-exclusion","category":"Equities","prompt":"A major sovereign wealth fund announces it will divest all fossil fuel holdings. What is the likely market impact on oil company stocks?","options":["Modest near-term selling pressure, but limited long-run effect as others buy in","An immediate and permanent collapse in the valuations of the whole oil sector","A sharp rally, as the remaining investors benefit from the concentrated selling","No effect whatsoever — large institutional selling never actually moves prices"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Divestment creates selling pressure, which can temporarily depress prices. But markets are generally efficient — if oil companies remain profitable, other investors (value funds, pension funds in non-ESG jurisdictions) step in to buy the \"cheap\" shares. The debate continues: divestment may raise the cost of capital for fossil fuel companies over time, even if immediate price impacts are small."},{"id":"sc-macro-surprise","category":"Macro","prompt":"Non-farm payrolls print 350,000 — far above the 180,000 consensus. Markets sell off. Why might a strong jobs number cause stocks to fall?","options":["A strong labour market cuts rate-cut odds, lifting yields and lowering multiples","More jobs means higher wages, which always crushes corporate profit margins at once","Investors actually prefer weak data, because it means lower government tax revenue","The stock market simply always falls on any strong economic data, as a rule"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"\"Good news is bad news\" is a common market dynamic when the Fed is in a tightening cycle. Strong jobs data means the central bank is less likely to cut rates — or may hike further. Higher expected rates push bond yields up and compress equity multiples (the \"rate hike premium\"). This is why the market reaction to data depends heavily on the current monetary policy context."},{"id":"sc-leveraged-etf-decay","category":"Equities","prompt":"A 3× leveraged ETF tracks the S&P 500. On Day 1 the index falls 10%; on Day 2 it rises 10%. What happens to a $1,000 investment in the leveraged ETF?","options":["Less than $1,000 — daily rebalancing causes volatility decay even on a flat index","Exactly $1,000 — the equal-sized gain and loss cancel each other out perfectly","Around $1,060 — the 3× leverage amplifies the small net gain over the two days","About $940 — a 10% fall then a 10% rise always leaves you with a net loss"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The index: 100 × 0.9 × 1.1 = 99 (-1%). The 3× ETF: Day 1 falls 30% ($1,000 → $700); Day 2 rises 30% ($700 → $910). The ETF loses 9% while the index lost only 1%. This \"volatility decay\" (also called beta slippage) makes leveraged ETFs poor long-term hold instruments — they are designed for short-term tactical use."},{"id":"sc-spin-off","category":"Equities","prompt":"A conglomerate announces it will spin off its fast-growing software division as a separate listed company. What typically happens to the parent's stock?","options":["It often re-rates upward as the spin-off unlocks hidden sum-of-parts value","It always falls afterwards, because the parent has permanently given away assets","It is entirely unaffected, since spin-offs are purely internal accounting moves","It falls steadily until the spin-off completes, then snaps back sharply higher"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Conglomerates often trade at a discount to the standalone values of their parts. Spinning off a high-growth division that was masked by legacy businesses lets each unit be valued by appropriate investors and on the right multiple. Academic research consistently finds spin-offs create value for both parent and spun-off entity in the two years following the transaction."},{"id":"sc-duration-mismatch","category":"Rates","prompt":"A bank funds itself with short-term deposits and holds long-term fixed-rate mortgages. Interest rates rise sharply. What risk has materialised?","options":["Duration mismatch — paying higher deposit rates while earning old fixed mortgage rates","Credit risk — the bank's mortgage borrowers begin to default because rates have risen","Liquidity risk — the mortgage assets on the book suddenly become impossible to sell","Currency risk — rising domestic rates drive up the bank's foreign funding costs"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is precisely what happened to Silicon Valley Bank in 2023. SVB held long-dated fixed-rate bonds funded by short-term deposits. When rates rose, its funding costs jumped while asset yields were locked in. The mark-to-market losses on the bond portfolio, combined with a run on deposits, caused the bank to fail. Duration mismatch is a fundamental interest rate risk in banking."},{"id":"sc-ipo-lockup","category":"Equities","prompt":"Six months after a tech company's IPO, the 180-day lockup period expires. What typically happens to the share price around this date?","options":["It often falls, as insiders and early backers are finally free to sell their shares","It rallies, because the company's shares have suddenly become far more liquid","It is unaffected — the lockup expiry was fully priced in months ahead of the date","It doubles, because demand from new investors far outstrips the added share supply"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Lockup agreements prevent insiders, founders, and pre-IPO investors from selling immediately after listing. When lockups expire, the float increases and previously restricted shareholders may sell — particularly if the stock has risen and they want to take profits. Empirically, stocks tend to underperform the market in the weeks around lockup expiry."},{"id":"sc-mezzanine","category":"Credit","prompt":"In a leveraged buyout capital structure, mezzanine debt sits between senior secured loans and equity. What does this mean for its risk and return?","options":["Riskier than senior debt — paid last — but higher yield, often with equity warrants","The same seniority as senior secured debt, yet it pays a distinctly lower coupon","Safer than senior debt, because being subordinated means it actually gets paid first","Equity-level risk paired, oddly, with plain bond-level returns and no upside at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Mezzanine fills the gap between cheap senior bank debt and expensive equity. In a liquidation, senior lenders are made whole first; mezzanine holders are paid next; equity gets whatever remains. This higher risk demands higher yield. Mezzanine often comes with \"equity kickers\" — warrants that convert to equity if the deal succeeds, capturing upside on top of the coupon."},{"id":"sc-passive-flows","category":"Equities","prompt":"A stock is added to the S&P 500 index. Passive funds tracking the index must now buy the stock. What typically happens to its price before and after the addition?","options":["Price rises as traders front-run the forced buying, then often fades after inclusion","Price falls, because index inclusion means far more competition for the company","Price is unaffected — passive funds trade so gradually the market never notices","Price rises indefinitely, since passive funds essentially never sell a constituent"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Index inclusion is a well-documented anomaly. Knowing that passive funds must buy a newly added stock, active traders buy in advance, pushing the price up. Once the forced buying is complete, the \"index premium\" often partially reverses. Stocks removed from the index show the mirror image. This is one way active traders exploit the predictable behaviour of passive investors."},{"id":"sc-current-account","category":"Macro","prompt":"The US runs a persistent current account deficit. This means the US is…","options":["Importing more than it exports, with the gap funded by foreign capital inflows","Printing money to pay for its imports, which causes automatic domestic inflation","Technically bankrupt already, and unable to service its outstanding foreign debts","Growing far faster than every surplus country in the world combined, all at once"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A current account deficit means a country buys more from the world than it sells. By accounting identity, this must be financed by capital inflows — foreigners invest in the deficit country (buying its bonds, equities, or real assets). The US can sustain a large deficit because the dollar is the global reserve currency, making dollar assets uniquely desirable to foreign investors."},{"id":"sc-leverage-margin-call","category":"Markets","prompt":"An investor uses 2:1 leverage to buy $200,000 of stock, putting up $100,000 of their own cash. The stock falls 20%. What happens?","options":["Equity falls to $60,000 — a 40% loss — and a margin call may follow","They lose exactly 20% on their capital, the same as an unleveraged position would","The broker quietly absorbs any loss above the investor's initial margin deposit","Nothing happens to them — leverage only ever amplifies gains, never the losses"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"$200,000 of stock falls 20% = $40,000 loss. But the $100,000 borrowed remains owed regardless. So equity drops from $100,000 to $60,000 — a 40% loss on capital. If equity falls below the broker's maintenance margin threshold, a margin call requires the investor to top up cash or the broker force-sells the position at the worst possible time."},{"id":"sc-velocity-money","category":"Macro","prompt":"The Fed quadruples the monetary base after the 2008 financial crisis but inflation stays low. What explains this?","options":["Money velocity collapsed — banks hoarded reserves, so the new money never circulated","The additional money supply the Fed created was immediately destroyed by deflation","Inflation is driven only by government fiscal spending, never by monetary policy","Global trade flows quietly absorbed all the excess money before it could hit prices"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"MV = PQ: inflation requires both money supply (M) and velocity (V — how fast money changes hands) to be high. Post-2008, banks held excess reserves at the Fed (earning interest on reserves for the first time) rather than lending. Velocity plummeted, so enormous QE produced little inflation — until COVID-era fiscal transfers put money directly in consumers' hands and velocity recovered."},{"id":"sc-oil-contango","category":"Commodities","prompt":"Oil futures are in contango — the price for delivery in 12 months is higher than the spot price today. What does this typically signal?","options":["Ample near-term supply, so the market pays a premium for later delivery","A firm signal that the spot price will always rise to meet the futures price later","A market expectation that oil demand is about to collapse all the way to zero","A quirk that only ever happens in the gold market, and essentially never in oil"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Contango happens when storage/financing costs plus expectations push future prices above spot — often when near-term supply is ample. The opposite, backwardation (futures below spot), suggests near-term scarcity. Producers can effectively \"lock in\" contango prices by storing oil and selling it forward."},{"id":"sc-gold-safe-haven","category":"Commodities","prompt":"Equity markets sell off sharply on recession fears. Gold rallies 4% the same week even though it pays no yield. Why?","options":["Gold is a safe haven — demand rises when investors want to preserve capital","Gold reliably moves in the very same direction as equities, week in and week out","Gold only ever rallies during periods when nominal interest rates are actually rising","Central banks step in and temporarily ban all gold trading during equity selloffs"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Gold has no cash flow, so it isn't valued like a stock or bond — it's held as a store of value and hedge against uncertainty. In risk-off periods investors often rotate into gold (and other havens like Treasuries and the yen) even while giving up yield elsewhere."},{"id":"sc-wheat-weather","category":"Commodities","prompt":"A severe drought hits a major wheat-growing region right before harvest. What is the most likely near-term effect on wheat futures?","options":["Prices rise sharply — expected supply falls while demand stays about the same","Prices fall, because the region's farmers rush to panic-sell their remaining crop","No real effect, since wheat is a globally fungible and easily substituted commodity","Prices stay fixed by international treaty, regardless of how small the harvest is"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Agricultural commodities are especially sensitive to weather because supply can't be quickly replaced mid-season. A drought cutting expected yields tightens supply against steady demand, pushing futures prices higher — a classic \"weather premium\" event."},{"id":"sc-commodity-supercycle","category":"Commodities","prompt":"Analysts argue copper is entering a \"supercycle\" due to demand from electric vehicles and grid infrastructure. What distinguishes a supercycle from a normal price rally?","options":["A multi-year structural demand or supply shift that keeps prices elevated for years","The price of the commodity roughly doubling within a single day of frantic trading","A market phenomenon that, historically, has only ever applied to the gold price","A commodity moving to trade exclusively on futures exchanges rather than spot"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Supercycles are driven by structural shifts — sustained demand growth (electrification needs far more copper) or supply constraints that take years to fix (new mines take a decade to develop). They're distinguished from short-lived spikes by their multi-year duration and structural, not just cyclical, drivers."},{"id":"sc-opec-compliance","category":"Commodities","prompt":"OPEC+ agrees to cut oil production to support prices, but several member countries are widely suspected of pumping above their quotas. Why does this matter for the price impact of the announced cut?","options":["With low compliance, real supply falls less than the headline cut, muting the impact","Cheating on the quotas always makes the eventual price impact larger than announced","OPEC members are, in practice, physically incapable of ever exceeding their quotas","Compliance is irrelevant, since prices are set purely on the futures exchanges anyway"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"OPEC+ quotas are a cartel agreement, not law — individual members have an incentive to cheat if the higher price makes extra barrels profitable. Markets watch compliance data closely, because a cut with poor compliance delivers much less real supply reduction than the headline number implies."},{"id":"sc-spr-release","category":"Commodities","prompt":"To fight high gasoline prices, the government announces a release of oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve (SPR) onto the market. What is the intended effect, and why does it usually fade?","options":["It adds supply to lower prices, but the reserve is finite, so the effect fades","It permanently fixes the oil price at a level chosen and defended by the government","It deliberately reduces available oil supply, which is intended to push prices higher","It has essentially no market effect at all, since the reserve is purely symbolic"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Strategic Petroleum Reserve is an emergency government stockpile. Releasing oil adds supply and can cool prices in the short run, but reserves are finite — sustained demand or tight supply reasserts itself once releases stop, and the government eventually needs to buy oil back to refill it."},{"id":"sc-commodity-etf-roll","category":"Commodities","prompt":"An investor buys an oil ETF that holds front-month futures contracts, expecting to track the oil price. Spot oil is flat over a year, but the ETF loses 15% to \"roll cost.\" What causes this?","options":["The market is in contango, so each monthly roll sells low and buys higher","The ETF's manager is quietly pocketing the difference as an undisclosed extra fee","Oil ETFs simply always lose money, regardless of the underlying market structure","A flat 15% government tax is automatically applied to all commodity ETF holdings"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Commodity ETFs holding futures (rather than physical barrels) must \"roll\" contracts before expiry — selling the expiring month and buying the next. In contango, the next month is pricier, so this roll is a structural drag (\"negative roll yield\") that can make the ETF underperform spot prices significantly over time."},{"id":"sc-circuit-breaker","category":"Markets","prompt":"The S&P 500 falls 7% intraday, triggering a market-wide trading halt. What is the purpose of this circuit breaker?","options":["To pause trading so the market can absorb information and avoid disorderly selling","To permanently close the exchange down for the remainder of that calendar year","To all but guarantee that the market reopens at a higher level than it halted at","To automatically cancel and unwind every single trade executed earlier that day"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"US exchanges use tiered circuit breakers (7%, 13%, 20% declines in the S&P 500) that trigger trading halts of increasing length. They exist to interrupt panic selling and give participants time to process news — not to reverse the market's direction."},{"id":"sc-etf-nav-arbitrage","category":"Markets","prompt":"An ETF's market price trades noticeably above the net asset value (NAV) of its underlying holdings. What keeps this gap from persisting?","options":["Authorized participants create new shares from the basket and sell them, closing the gap","Nothing — an ETF's price can permanently diverge from NAV with no correcting force","The government steps in and fixes ETF prices each day to match their stated NAV","ETFs are, by law, strictly prohibited from ever trading above their underlying NAV"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"ETFs have a creation/redemption mechanism: authorized participants can exchange a basket of the underlying securities for new ETF shares (or vice versa). If the ETF trades above NAV, they profit by creating shares cheaply and selling at the market price — arbitrage that keeps the ETF closely tied to its underlying NAV."},{"id":"sc-dual-class-shares","category":"Markets","prompt":"A founder holds Class B shares with 10 votes each, while public investors hold Class A shares with 1 vote each. What is the main implication of this structure?","options":["The founder keeps voting control while owning only a minority of the economics","All shareholders in fact hold identical voting power, regardless of their share class","The public Class A shares are legally barred from ever receiving any dividends","Dual-class share structures are, in fact, illegal on all major US stock exchanges"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Dual-class structures (used by companies like Meta and Alphabet, and many family-controlled firms) let founders raise public capital while keeping outsized voting control, because voting rights and economic ownership are decoupled. Critics argue this weakens accountability to public shareholders; supporters argue it protects long-term strategy from short-term pressure."},{"id":"sc-payment-order-flow","category":"Markets","prompt":"A \"commission-free\" brokerage routes its customers' stock orders to a market maker, which pays the brokerage for the right to fill those orders. What is this arrangement called?","options":["Payment for order flow — the market maker pays the broker instead of the customer","A standard prime brokerage agreement between the broker and a large institution","A routine dividend reinvestment plan operated on behalf of the retail customers","A margin call, triggered automatically once the order is routed to the market maker"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Payment for order flow (PFOF) is how many \"free\" brokerages actually make money: market makers pay for the right to execute retail orders, profiting from the bid-ask spread. Regulators scrutinize it because the broker's incentive (maximize PFOF revenue) doesn't always perfectly align with getting the customer the best possible execution price."},{"id":"sc-ex-dividend-drop","category":"Markets","prompt":"A stock trades at $100 the day before its ex-dividend date, and is due to pay a $2 dividend. All else equal, what happens to the share price when it opens ex-dividend?","options":["Around $98 — the $2 dividend is deducted since new buyers no longer receive it","It stays at exactly $100, because a dividend has no mechanical effect on the price","It rises by the full dividend amount, opening at roughly $102 on the ex-date","It is required, under exchange rules, to double in price on the ex-dividend day"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"On the ex-dividend date, anyone buying the stock no longer receives the upcoming dividend — only existing holders as of the prior \"record date\" do. So the exchange mechanically adjusts the opening reference price down by roughly the dividend amount, reflecting that the cash is about to leave the company."},{"id":"sc-block-trade","category":"Markets","prompt":"A pension fund needs to sell $200 million of a stock. Instead of dumping it on the public order book, it arranges a privately negotiated \"block trade\" with an investment bank. Why?","options":["Dumping that size openly would move the price against the seller; a block avoids that","Block trades are simply required by law for any single sale larger than $1 million","Arranging it as a block trade lets the fund avoid paying any capital gains tax due","A block trade guarantees a higher price than the public market could ever offer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Large orders can overwhelm the available liquidity in the public order book, pushing the price down as the order fills (market impact). Investment banks facilitate block trades — negotiating a single price directly with the seller, often taking the position onto their own book — to execute size with far less price disruption than working it through the open market."},{"id":"sc-insider-blackout","category":"Markets","prompt":"A public company's employees are barred from trading its stock for several weeks before each quarterly earnings release. What is this restriction designed to prevent?","options":["Trading on material non-public information before it is released to the public","Stopping employees from ever selling their fully vested stock at any point in a year","Preventing the company itself from reporting its quarterly earnings figures on time","Barring ordinary retail investors from trading the stock at all during that window"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A \"blackout period\" restricts insiders (employees, executives, directors) from trading around earnings because they may know results before the public does — trading on that would be illegal insider trading. It's a compliance safeguard, not a restriction on the stock's trading generally; anyone without inside information can still trade freely."},{"id":"sc-put-protection","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"An investor is worried about a near-term market drop but doesn't want to sell their stock. They buy a put option on the index instead. What does this achieve?","options":["It caps their downside — the put gains value if the market falls, for a premium","It effectively guarantees that the underlying stock position will keep rising in value","It removes every trace of risk from the position, and does so at no upfront cost","It obligates the investor to sell their entire stock holding immediately at the strike"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A put option gives the right (not obligation) to sell at a set strike price. If the market falls below the strike, the put's value rises, offsetting losses on the underlying position — like an insurance policy paid for with the option premium. If the market doesn't fall, that premium is simply the cost of the protection."},{"id":"sc-covered-call","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"An investor owns 100 shares of a stock and sells a call option against them for extra income (a \"covered call\"). What is the main trade-off?","options":["They collect premium but cap upside — a big rally forces them to sell at the strike","They eliminate all of the downside risk on the underlying long stock position outright","They become obligated to go out and buy another 100 shares of the same stock at once","The strategy only actually works in the case where the stock price never moves at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Selling a covered call generates premium income immediately, but if the stock rises above the strike, the buyer will exercise the call and the seller must deliver shares at that (now below-market) price — capping the upside. It's a popular income strategy for investors willing to trade away some upside for steady premium."},{"id":"sc-interest-rate-swap","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"A company has floating-rate debt and is worried about rising rates. It enters an interest rate swap to pay a fixed rate and receive floating. What has it achieved?","options":["It has turned floating-rate debt into a fixed obligation, hedging rate increases","It has, in effect, doubled its total exposure to moves in the underlying interest rate","It has completely eliminated the underlying debt itself from its balance sheet entirely","It now simply receives the full notional principal amount of the swap upfront in cash"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In a pay-fixed/receive-floating swap, the floating payments the company receives offset the floating payments it owes on its actual debt, leaving it effectively paying a fixed rate. This is a common way for companies and banks to manage interest rate risk without refinancing the underlying loan."},{"id":"sc-cds-protection","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"An investor holds a corporate bond and buys a credit default swap (CDS) referencing that issuer. What does the CDS provide?","options":["Protection against the issuer defaulting — the seller pays out on a credit event","A guaranteed higher coupon payment on the underlying corporate bond that it references","Ownership of a block of additional shares in the referenced company, free of charge","The automatic conversion of the underlying bond into equity upon any credit event"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A CDS is essentially default insurance: the buyer pays a periodic premium, and if the referenced issuer defaults or has a defined credit event, the seller pays out (or takes delivery of the defaulted bond at par). Investors use CDS to hedge credit risk, and traders use them to speculate on credit quality without owning the underlying bond."},{"id":"sc-futures-vs-forward","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"A company wants to lock in the price of a future commodity purchase, negotiate exact contract terms, and doesn't need it exchange-traded. Should it use a futures contract or a forward contract?","options":["A forward — a private, customizable OTC deal, unlike a standardized exchange future","A futures contract, because listed futures always allow fully customized private terms","Neither one — locking in a future purchase price simply is not possible in practice","A stock option, since an equity option works in an essentially identical way to this"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Futures are standardized, exchange-traded, and marked to market daily with a clearinghouse guaranteeing performance. Forwards are private, customizable OTC contracts between two counterparties, settled at maturity — offering flexibility on terms at the cost of counterparty risk and less liquidity."},{"id":"sc-implied-volatility","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"Ahead of a company's earnings report, the price of its options rises sharply even though the stock price itself hasn't moved. What is driving this?","options":["Rising implied volatility — the market expects a big move on the earnings report","The company has just announced a routine forward stock split, lifting option demand","Interest rates across the curve have suddenly fallen all the way down to about zero","Nothing real — option prices are actually unrelated to any expected future volatility"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Option prices are highly sensitive to implied volatility — the market's expectation of how much the underlying will move. Ahead of earnings, uncertainty about the outcome rises, so implied volatility (and option premiums) rise even with no change in the stock price itself — which is also why option prices often fall sharply right after earnings (\"vol crush\") once that uncertainty resolves."},{"id":"sc-option-expiry-worthless","category":"Derivatives","prompt":"An investor buys a call option that expires out of the money (the stock never reaches the strike price). What is their maximum possible loss?","options":["The premium they paid — nothing more, since option buyers have capped downside","Potentially unlimited losses, exactly the same as if they had been short the stock","They must still go ahead and buy the underlying stock at the strike price regardless","They now owe the option seller an additional margin payment to close the position"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Buying an option (call or put) has a defined maximum loss: the premium paid. If it expires worthless, the buyer simply loses that premium — unlike selling options, which can carry unlimited (for calls) or substantial (for puts) risk. This asymmetry is why buying options is sometimes described as \"limited downside, uncapped upside.\""},{"id":"sc-central-bank-intervention","category":"FX","prompt":"A country's currency is falling rapidly. Its central bank sells foreign reserves (like US dollars) to buy its own currency in the open market. What is the goal?","options":["To support the currency by directly buying it, slowing or reversing the fall","To deliberately make the currency fall even faster than it already is falling now","To increase the country's domestic money supply and stimulate its slowing economy","To engineer a deliberate default on the country's outstanding foreign-currency debt"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"When a central bank sells foreign-currency reserves to buy its own currency, it directly boosts demand for the domestic currency, supporting its value. This tool is limited, though — reserves are finite, and speculators can overwhelm intervention if they believe the currency is fundamentally overvalued."},{"id":"sc-purchasing-power-parity","category":"FX","prompt":"Over the long run, a country with persistently higher inflation than its trading partners tends to see its currency…","options":["Depreciate — under PPP, rates adjust so goods cost about the same across borders","Appreciate steadily and indefinitely, entirely regardless of its higher inflation rate","Stay perfectly fixed forever against its trading partners, whatever inflation does","Automatically become the world's dominant reserve currency, simply through inflation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Purchasing power parity (PPP) theory holds that if a country's prices rise faster than its trading partners', its currency should depreciate to keep the real price of goods roughly equal across borders. It's a long-run tendency rather than a precise short-term predictor — but it explains why persistently high-inflation countries tend to see currency weakness over time."},{"id":"sc-fx-trade-deficit","category":"FX","prompt":"A country runs a large and growing trade deficit, importing far more than it exports, and — unlike the US — does not issue the world's reserve currency. All else equal, what pressure does this put on its currency?","options":["Downward pressure — more currency is sold to buy imports than the other way round","Upward pressure, since a persistent trade deficit always signals a very strong economy","No pressure at all — cross-border trade flows have no bearing on currency values","It guarantees the currency will soon be formally fixed to a gold standard again"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A trade deficit means residents sell their own currency to buy foreign goods and currency more than foreigners buy the domestic currency. Without offsetting capital inflows — which a reserve-currency issuer like the US can attract far more easily — sustained deficits tend to pressure a currency lower over time."},{"id":"sc-currency-peg-break","category":"FX","prompt":"A country maintains a fixed exchange rate (peg) to the US dollar, but is burning through foreign reserves defending it as speculators bet the peg is unsustainable. If the central bank eventually abandons the peg, what typically happens?","options":["The currency drops sharply and suddenly, free to find its weaker clearing level","The currency automatically appreciates the moment it is finally allowed to float freely","Nothing much changes, since a currency peg has no real economic effect in the first place","An international body steps in and permanently re-fixes the rate at the old peg level"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Countries defend pegs by spending FX reserves to buy their own currency. If reserves run out — or the peg is seen as unsustainably overvalued — the central bank must let the currency float, usually resulting in a sharp, sudden devaluation, since the peg had been holding it above its market-clearing rate. The 1997 Asian Financial Crisis is a classic example."}];
